# Blood of the Lioness

> GM-facing narrative for the adventure *Blood of the Lioness: An Adventure at the Battle of Snow
> Plain* (L5R15DLC01a, Fantasy Flight Games). Boxed **read-aloud** text is reproduced verbatim as
> blockquotes; intervening GM procedure (check TNs, mass-battle bookkeeping, intrigue momentum) is
> summarized faithfully — the full detail lives in the source PDF. Statblocks are in
> `l5r5e-0.4-blood-of-the-lioness-cast.ttrpg`, the new invocation and distinction in
> `l5r5e-0.4-blood-of-the-lioness-mechanics.ttrpg`, and the three-part structure in
> `l5r5e-0.4-blood-of-the-lioness.arc`. The players use their own characters; in Part Two they also
> play historical figures (premade Advisors or their own historical selves), optionally linked to
> their present PCs by a **Legacy** — a special advantage from the external *Blood of the Lioness*
> online supplement, not defined in this booklet.

*Blood of the Lioness* is a military-themed adventure in which the PCs hear and experience
conflicting stories about a critical battle in the Lion's past — the **Battle of Snow Plain** (820) —
and the truths they discover could change how the past is understood and how the future unfolds. The
sourcebook *Fields of Victory* is an excellent resource for expanding or continuing it.

The nature of the **vision** in Part Two is left to the GM: a grounded shared spiritual experience in
which the PCs' actions do not alter the timeline, a genuine mental transportation to the past in
which they can "alter" details, or something deliberately ambiguous. Whatever it is, it is called a
*vision* throughout.

---

## Part One: The Official Record (Castle of the Swift Sword, 1123)

The PCs are summoned to the **Akodo War College**, in the **Castle of the Swift Sword**, to gather
information on a dispute over a **diamond mine** in Unicorn territory that the **Scorpion** claim was
promised to them for their aid at the Battle of Snow Plain in 820, three hundred years earlier. They
are to report to the appointed magistrate **Kitsuki Kāgi**. By default the PCs are Emerald Magistrates
sent by acting Emerald Champion **Agasha Sumiko**; the *Player Character Motives* section offers
clan-specific hooks (Crab: old agreements to reinforce the Wall; Crane: strengthen a claim to the
Osari Plains; Dragon: ninjō or lordly tasks; Lion: protect the clan's name as keepers of history;
Phoenix: the Toturi–Kaede betrothal; Scorpion: ensure a favorable finding; Unicorn: keep the mine
under Unicorn control; plus generic and Minor Clan motives).

**A Warm Welcome.** At the gates the PCs are greeted by the Kitsu daimyō's advisor **Kitsu Yayoi**:

> A lithe man in fine robes bows and hails you as you approach the front gates. "Greetings, honored
> guests. I am Kitsu Yayoi, and I will be helping you settle in this evening. The daimyō sends her
> regrets, but she is unable to meet with you until the morning to discuss access to the more
> restricted areas of the libraries. For now, though, I will be happy to show you the grounds. I
> imagine you are hungry, too."

When the PCs ask after Kitsuki Kāgi, Yayoi explains the magistrate has been delayed:

> We have received word that Kitsuki Kāgi, the magistrate who will be rendering the final judgment,
> has been waylaid by unseasonable rains, and does not expect to arrive for several days. Shosuro
> Aishi and Ide Tsubame, the representatives of the Scorpion and Unicorn, are traveling with him.
> However, he sends word that you should begin your research and await his arrival.

**A Three-Hour Tour.** Crossing the parade grounds, the PCs meet the gruff drill sergeant **Akodo
Atsushi** (Loyal Bushi with the Large Stature distinction), who is surprised to be tasked as a guide:

> At the far end of the courtyard, a group of warriors follows the orders of its towering drill
> sergeant. Abruptly, the sergeant's shouting ceases. They bow to their students, ending the lesson.
> Your guide leads you toward the drill sergeant, smiling and gesturing to the gruff Lion samurai.
> "Atsushi-sama, a moment of your time. We have guests," he begins, introducing each of you in turn.
> Akodo Atsushi salutes you warily before Yayoi continues, "Please show them the library at the War
> College, sergeant. You are very familiar with the War College, after all!"

PCs may notice Atsushi's veiled frustration (TN 2 Sentiment, Air 1 Earth 3); offering to find the way
themselves, or persuading Yayoi to let him remain, earns honor for Compassion (Jin). Unknown to
Atsushi, he carries his ancestor **Akodo Aoi's** tenugui and is connected to the battle.

**The Castle and the War College.** The Castle of the Swift Sword — above the Drowned Merchant River
and the Plains of Bloodied Honor — is jointly held by the **Kitsu** and **Akodo** families, houses the
Tomb of the Five and the secret **Golden Lantern Dōjō**, and adjoins the **Ikoma Hall of Scribes**.

**The Military Library.** Atsushi leaves the PCs with the peasant-born librarian **Kitsu Ayako**:

> Only one figure moves, a slight and hesitant young woman dressed in scholar's robes. "Welcome to
> the library," she says, bowing. "I am Kitsu Ayako. It is a pleasure to meet you. Daimyō Takeko sent
> word of your arrival, and I am grateful to assist you however I can."

A PC with Theology who makes a TN 3 Theology (Void) check glimpses a shimmering armored shape near
Ayako — the shade of **Kitsu Yū**, Ayako's unknown ancestor, whom neither Ayako nor most others can
perceive. Ayako directs the PCs to the official scroll of the battle, signed by a Kitsu named **Yū**:

- The Lion once ruled the Snow Plain, but when the Unicorn returned in 815 the Emperor granted the
  plain and other territories to the Unicorn.
- In 820, embittered by the loss, the Lion instigated the Battle of Snow Plain to retake the land.
- The renowned Lion general **Matsu Sakura** confronted the Unicorn general **Shinjo Kamu**, but was
  surprised: prepared for mounted warriors, she instead faced foot soldiers, for the Unicorn had
  secretly sought Scorpion aid in exchange for rights to defunct mines, and defeated the overconfident
  Matsu.

The scroll **omits** that Kitsu Yū invoked her ancestors, who charged into battle beside the Lion —
the truth that keeps Yū's shade from resting (*A Restless Spirit*). PCs may explore further, meet the
elderly historian **Kitsu Taigen** (Ayako's adoptive father) and the young daimyō **Kitsu Takeko**,
and be shown the printing of *Akodo's Leadership*.

**Akodo's Leadership and Student of Akodo.** A non-Lion character who spends a downtime activity
studying the manuscript and succeeds at a TN 4 Composition or Culture (Void) check may purchase the
new distinction **Student of Akodo (Void)** [Interpersonal, Mental] for 3 XP (see the mechanics file).
Its effect:

> You have studied the famous text known as Akodo's Leadership, which has given you an insight into
> Lion culture. You can identify the proper purpose of all Rokugani battlefield weapons, even if you
> are not personally proficient in their use, and you know about most common military maneuvers and
> engagements, especially those preferred by the Lion Clan. When making a check to understand the Lion
> Clan mindset or strategy (such as a Culture [Void] check to intuit the correct way to broach a
> difficult subject to a Lion Clan character of higher status or Tactics [Void] check to predict what
> strategy a Lion Clan general will employ), you may reroll up to two dice.

**The Ikoma Hall of Scribes.** Following the record that names **Ikoma Hideri** as the historian
assigned to Sakura's army, the PCs visit the Hall of Scribes:

> You enter a vast hall, silent but for the whisper of a scroll being unrolled with reverence and your
> own footsteps echoing on the wooden floor. A wooden tile in the center depicts the Ikoma family
> crest. The Kitsu, Akodo, and Matsu crests mark the paths to the wings of those families. A scholar
> bends over a scroll at the far end of the room, but there may be others hidden among the shelves or
> in the adjoining rooms.

The Ikoma bard **Ikoma Ichigo** can help; Hideri's own account of the battle should be here, but there
is nothing to find. Confronted with the *Historical Inconsistencies* between the Ikoma and Akodo
records — and the missing truth — the PCs place an offering at the castle's **ancestral shrine**:

> As soon as you place the offering, the candle flickers. Suddenly, a woman in heavy armor appears
> behind the altar, clutching her katana with one hand and a fresh wound in her side with the other.
> She looks almost real, but you can see the wall through her, and she does not seem to see you with
> her glowing golden eyes—the eyes of a great hunting cat. She opens her mouth to speak, and then she
> vanishes.

This draws the PCs into the vision of the Battle of Snow Plain.

---

## Part Two: The Vision (Battle of Snow Plain, 820)

The PCs take the roles of historical figures — premade **Advisors** or their own historical selves,
optionally tied to their present PCs by a **Legacy** — advising the Lion general **Matsu Sakura**
against the Unicorn general **Shinjo Kamu**. The **Personal Histories** sidebar contrasts recorded
history with the truths the PCs will uncover: Sakura was recorded as virtuous and victorious; Kitsu
Yū as a loyal bushi with untapped shugenja potential (Ayako's ancestor); Ikoma Hideri as a meticulous,
courageous chronicler whose work was lost through no fault of her own; the Scorpion emissary **Shosuro
Amane** did make a deal with the Unicorn but never kept the peace; and a shinobi, **Shosuro Yasu**
(impersonating the medic Akodo Kamayoko), caused Hideri's death — her name kept out of the histories.

**Clan Motives (820).** The Lion mean to drive the Unicorn from the Snow Plain (Champion Ikoma Kojima
sent Sakura); the Unicorn seek to prove themselves worthy of the land; and the Scorpion, invited to
mediate, secretly want the diamond wealth of the mountains.

**Overview of the Battle (four days).** Day 1: the PCs advise Sakura and lead cohorts. Day 2: a
snowstorm; Sakura attempts a surprise attack and asks the PCs to root out a spy. Day 3: the sky clears,
Unicorn reinforcements arrive, and Yū's powers surface. Day 4: Sakura would use Yū to claim victory,
but an Imperial emissary arrives and changes history; Kamu challenges Sakura to a duel, and a trusted
advisor takes her place. Then the vision ends.

**Day 1 — Playing Sakura's Advisors.** All leaders begin with 4 momentum toward the ongoing social
objective **Maintain the General's Favor**, which tracks Sakura's trust over the four days (8+ trusted
absolutely; 4–7 in good standing; 1–3 out of favor; 0 dismissed). In the war council Sakura weighs
whether to attack at once or wait; the PCs' advice shifts both her decision and her regard for them.
On **The First Sparks** (the strategic objective *Test Their Mettle*, fulfilled at momentum equal to
Kamu's focus, 7), the Unicorn are revealed as unmounted spearmen amid boggy paddies:

> Suddenly, the forest of spears begins to draw back across the marsh – not in a panic, but in a slow,
> deliberate withdrawal, as if reacting to some unseen signal. You see Matsu Sakura questioning the
> historian Ikoma Hideri on a nearby hilltop, her muddy scholar's robes an odd sight before the sea
> of shining lacquered armor and glinting spear tips. As you approach the pair, you catch the end of
> their conversation, Hideri's voice steady despite her harried appearance. "…this tactic is attested
> to in Kuni Kagetsugu's account of the skirmish at the Ridge of Bones. If the Unicorn appear to be
> in retreat, then what they desire is that we pursue. We should hold our position, and move
> cautiously across this terrain."

If the Lion advance heedlessly into the concealed pit traps, they suffer 6 attrition and 12 panic. The
**Eyes of the Kitsu** sidebar seeds Yū's growing dizziness and visions; a PC who recognizes her
condition as **ancestral summoning** (TN 2 Theology, spending Opportunity) better prepares her for
Day 3 (*Yū's Intervention*).

**The Generals Converse / Seizing the Hilltop.** The Unicorn withdraw to a fortified hilltop; Kamu
rides out with the Scorpion **Shosuro Amane** and an honor guard of five spearmen:

> The Unicorn general does not dismount, but frowns down on Sakura. She holds her head high and
> ignores the insult, instead demanding that Kamu withdraw from the plain. The Unicorn abruptly
> refuses, but the Scorpion speaks, introducing themselves as Shosuro Amane. They have been brought
> along to negotiate peace, but as neither general is willing to back down, there will be no peace
> today.

The following mass-battle rounds contest the hilltop (a difficulty-7 fortification held by **Moto
Ghazan's** cohort). Interfering with the generals' exchange costs honor, glory, and Sakura's favor,
but lets the PCs size up Kamu and Amane.

**Day 2 — Spy in the Camp / The Five Pillars; Day 3 — Yū's Intervention.** A snowstorm and a
forewarned surprise attack lead Sakura to ask the PCs to find a spy — **Shosuro Yasu**, who murders
Ikoma Hideri. As the days pass Yū and Ayako commune ever more strongly with their ancestor spirits,
until on Day 3 Yū awakens as an **ancestral summoner**, calling Lion ancestors into the battle — the
discovery that later founds the Golden Lantern Dōjō and the **Ancestral Summons** invocation (see the
cast and mechanics files).

**Day 4 — The Duel.** With history turning on an Imperial intervention and Amane's treachery exposed,
Kamu demands a duel to the death. Sakura, her sword arm damaged, appeals to the PCs; Yū offers to
champion her, but a PC who offers to duel in her place gains honor. The duel closes the vision.

---

## Part Three: The Truth (Castle of the Swift Sword, 1123)

The PCs return to the present. Ayako warns that, while a Kitsu family audience might accept their
shared vision, most samurai would treat the tale with suspicion. In Rokugani law (*Status and Truth*)
the higher-status party is generally favored, and neither side has a single incontrovertible proof —
so Kitsuki Kāgi's interpretation of tangential testimony will decide who owns the mine.

**Preparing for the Arbitration.** The PCs have three days before Kāgi arrives, and may run one
downtime activity and one to two narrative scenes or intrigues per day to strengthen their case:

- **Gather Witness Testimony** — persuade a castle NPC to weigh in (an Appeal to a Person/Group
  objective); each witness convinced adds momentum equal to the PC's status rank.
- **Acquire Written Testimony** — a TN 3 Scholar downtime check to find substantiating documents (each
  ring usable once); each piece is worth 1 momentum plus 1 per bonus success in the arbitration.
- **Forge Written Testimony** — a TN 3 Composition (Air) check to fabricate a convincing document, at
  the cost of forfeited honor and staked glory; worth 1 momentum plus 3 per bonus success, but exposure
  (a TN 3 Composition or Government, Fire, check) costs all its momentum and the staked glory.
- **Other Clever Plots** — blackmail, bribery, or leverage over the claimants **Shosuro Aishi** and
  **Ide Tsubame**; each viable plan awards momentum.

**Rendering Judgment.** After three days the claimants arrive with Kāgi: **Shosuro Aishi**, a
descendant of Shosuro Amane whose discovery of an ancestral letter started the trouble, and **Ide
Tsubame**, an ambitious young Unicorn courtier and descendant of Shinjo Kamu. Kāgi summons the PCs and
interested NPCs to present their cases; he may be persuaded toward the Unicorn, the Scorpion, Imperial
control, or another faction, per the social-objective thresholds in the `.arc`. Unicorn and Scorpion
PCs must side with their own clan or forfeit glory (*Choosing Sides*), and how the PCs wield the truth
they uncovered determines whether they succeed in their mission or start a war — and who gains the
diamond mine.
